    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2a493632_c6bc5661",
        "filename": "chip/stm32/flash-f.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 8,
      "author": {
        "id": 1314845
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-07-12T16:29:56Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We have stdbool, so it seems like it should be fine to use it. Using a bool makes it clear to the reader that we only expect true/false.\n\nExample:\n\n int some_var;\n\n some_var \u003d some_func();\n\nvs\n\n  bool some_var;\n\n  some_var \u003d some_func();\n\nIn the latter I can easily tell that it\u0027s safe to write\n\n  if (some_var) {\n    // do something\n  }\n\nwithout worrying that some_func potentially returns negative values, which would be interpreted as true in the if statement.\n\nI\u0027ll ask on chatty-firmware to see if there are any other reasons it\u0027s not used. My guess is that it\u0027s just a side effect of using C89, which doesn\u0027t include it by default. (Another future project of mine is to switch to std\u003dC11 so that we can use static_assert: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C11_(C_standard_revision))",
      "parentUuid": "4c57b0de_bd3f42bd",
      "revId": "24323968eaed3efa8dd28fcf650780d139ae7b3b",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6f439700_f8784ebf",
        "filename": "chip/stm32/flash-f.c",
